<%# Shared SLA state filter pills (All / SLA Met / SLA Breached / At Risk / No SLA) for the %>
<%# ticket-level detail. Rendered BOTH beside the compact card heading and inside the expand modal; %>
<%# sla_dashboard_detail_table.js keeps the two copies in lockstep, so whichever one you click, both %>
<%# show the same active pill and the one table below them is filtered the same way. %>
<%# %>
<%# CLIENT-SIDE, like the search, sorting and pagination already are: the controller renders rows %>
<%# for EVERY state (SlaDashboardController#build_detail_table) and these buttons hide and show %>
<%# those rows in place. They used to be plain GET links that reloaded the whole page for a filter %>
<%# the browser already had every row for — the one control on this table that still did. %>
<%# %>
<%# Counts come from @counts, the single source every dashboard part reads, so they still describe %>
<%# the whole server scope rather than whatever is currently on screen. %>
<%# %>
<%# `.sla-pill` (partials/_pill_group.css) rather than utilities: it is the same "pick one of these" %>
<%# component the SLA Trend date presets use, and its whole reason for existing is that active state %>
<%# has to be ONE class the JS can toggle — toggling the utilities instead loses to the inactive %>
<%# state's own `hover:` variant on specificity, which is documented at length in that file. %>
<% detail_states = [['all', l(:label_sla_detail_state_all), @counts.total]] +
     %w[met breached at_risk no_sla].map { |s| [s, sla_card_label(s.to_sym), @counts.public_send(s)] } %>
<% detail_states.each do |state, label, count| %>
  <button type="button" data-sla-state-filter="<%= state %>"
          class="sla-pill<%= ' is-active' if state == @state_filter %>">
    <%= "#{label} (#{count})" %>
  </button>
<% end %>
